-- title:
--   A cube contains its own centre
-- statement:
--   For a side length $h > 0$ and any point $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$, the centre belongs to its cube:
--   $$u \in A_h(u),$$
--   where $A_h(u)$ is the open cube of side $h$ centred at $u$, i.e. the set of $x$ with $\|x-u\|_\infty < h/2$.
--
--   Trivial as it is, this is the nonemptiness statement for cubes and is what makes the cubic tiling a covering: it guarantees that each cube of the family carries at least one point, and in particular that a cube attached to a lattice site is never empty, so that averages over it are well defined.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Positivity of $h$ is necessary and is exactly what is assumed: for $h \le 0$ the cube is empty and the statement fails.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/LebesgueDiff.lean#L62-L68

